.TH SIGNUM\-ACCOUNT 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.3.2" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
signum\-account \- signum\-account < passphrase
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
 signum\-account < passphrase
 stdout: one JSON line of PUBLIC data: {"account_id","address":"S\-..."}. No secret is echoed.
 The passphrase is the exact secret; a single trailing newline (as a file adds) is stripped, nothing else.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
stdout: one JSON line of PUBLIC data: the numeric account id and the S\- Reed\-Solomon address.
The secret passphrase is read from stdin, hashed in memory (zeroized), and never echoed.
Burst/Signum derivation is NOT BIP39/secp256k1: passphrase \-> SHA\-256 private key \->
Curve25519 base multiplication (clamped, as libsodium/NXT) \-> public key \-> account id.
.SH OPTIONS
No flags. Input is read from stdin or the arguments shown in SYNOPSIS.
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
stdout: one JSON line of PUBLIC data: the numeric account id and the S\- Reed\-Solomon address.
The secret passphrase is read from stdin, hashed in memory (zeroized), and never echoed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.TP
2
last status bad_input
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ echo 'correct horse battery staple' | signum\-account
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ printf '' | signum\-account
# exit 2, last status: bad_input
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It may open hostile wallet files and handle secrets: run it sealed and offline (./blocktools run; sealed\-run allows it).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
.SH SEE ALSO
